<div class="docs-article">
  <h1>3. Fine-Grained Reactivity Engine</h1>
  <p class="lead-text">
    DriftJS uses a direct dependency indexing model combined with microtask change batching, bypassing virtual DOM overhead completely.
  </p>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <h2>Change Notification &amp; Microtask Batching</h2>
  <p>
    When a variable in the component scope is modified (e.g. <code>count++</code>), the VM invokes <code>markDirty('count')</code>. All dirty variables are collected in a <code>Set&lt;string&gt;</code> and processed in a single batched microtask via <code>queueMicrotask()</code>:
  </p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><code>// In DriftClientVM:
public markDirty(varName: string): void &#123;
  this.pendingDirtyVars.add(varName);
  if (!this.isUpdateScheduled) &#123;
    this.isUpdateScheduled = true;
    queueMicrotask(() =&gt; this.flushUpdates());
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Reactive Bindings &amp; In-Place DOM Patching</h2>
  <p>
    The compiler records a table of <code>ReactiveBinding</code> structures mapping variable names to exact program counter (<code>pc</code>) bytecode positions. When <code>triggerUpdates(changedVars)</code> runs:
  </p>
  <ul class="bullet-list">
    <li><strong><code>INTERPOLATE_TEXT</code></strong>: Updates <code>textNode.nodeValue</code> directly in O(1) time.</li>
    <li><strong><code>SET_ATTR</code></strong>: Mutates the element attribute, property, or style object in-place.</li>
    <li><strong><code>REACTIVE_IF</code> / <code>REACTIVE_FOR</code></strong>: Re-renders only the reactive subtree situated strictly between DOM comment delimiters.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
